0068 - esp32s31 AHB GDMA: four cyclic-TX (audio playback) fixes
===============================================================

Full copy of drivers/dma/esp32s31-ahb-gdma.c. Found by reading the driver
during the 48 kHz audio plan (docs/audio-48k-plan-2026-09-25.md), fixed
2026-09-25, kernel #387.

1. esp32s31_ahb_start_cyclic_tx() used the PERIOD index as a DESCRIPTOR
   index. A TX period may span several descriptors (per_period), so any
   start at a non-zero period landed mid-period. Now tx_node * per_period.
2. prep_dma_cyclic rounded the chunk DOWN, which could leave a 4-byte tail
   descriptor per period (8188 -> 4092 + 4092 + 4). Now the period is split
   evenly with the chunk rounded UP inside the aligned 4092-byte cap.
3. The position was an interrupt COUNT: two period-ends latched into one
   interrupt advanced it by one, and the reported position then stayed a
   period behind for the rest of the stream (ALSA believing more was queued
   than was). Now taken from the hardware: OUT_EOF_DES_ADDR (channel +0x9c,
   "address of the transmit descriptor when the EOF bit in this descriptor
   is 1", IDF ahb_dma_reg.h) -> period. A read-only counter
   /sys/module/esp32s31_ahb_gdma/parameters/tx_pos_fixups counts the
   corrections.
4. After a DMA error the ring restarted at node 0 while the client kept
   writing ahead - playback jumped back a buffer and the position went
   backwards. Now it restarts at the current period (needs fix 1).

VERIFIED, #387: boots clean; sdlquake plays (11025 Hz S16 stereo, 512-frame
periods) with tx_pos_fixups 0; a 48 kHz stream under two CPU0 spinners plus
25 MB of O_DIRECT SD writes: tx_pos_fixups 0, no cyclic TX error. So on this
board the coalescing case does not currently occur and fix 3 is insurance.
With 2 KB periods the code path is identical to before except one register
read per interrupt. No change can alter normal playback; an ear check is
simply playing anything.
